{"id":176141051596461,"type":"genre","name":"Funk industrial","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":16,"info":{"img":null},"own_scenes":[{"id":276,"name":"Funk industrial","book":{"id":4,"slug":"Funk_Vincent","title":"Funk: The Music, the People, and the Rhythm of the One","author":"Rickey Vincent","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/Funk/","families":{"raices":{"name":"Raíces: blues, jazz, gospel y R&B","color":"#d97706"},"soul":{"name":"Soul y proto-funk: Stax, Nueva Orleans, Motown","color":"#facc15"},"jb":{"name":"James Brown y los JB’s","color":"#ef4444"},"rock":{"name":"Funk psicodélico y black rock: Sly, Hendrix","color":"#c084fc"},"fusion":{"name":"Jazz-funk y fusión","color":"#38bdf8"},"bandas":{"name":"Bandas de funk de los setenta","color":"#22c55e"},"pfunk":{"name":"P-Funk: Parliament-Funkadelic","color":"#a3e635"},"disco":{"name":"Disco y dance funk","color":"#f472b6"},"ochenta":{"name":"Funk de los ochenta: Prince, Rick James, electro","color":"#22d3ee"},"hiphop":{"name":"Hip hop, go-go y funk de los noventa","color":"#94a3b8"},"cultura":{"name":"Política y cultura negra","color":"#e5e7eb"}}},"family":"Hip hop, go-go y funk de los noventa","color":"#94a3b8","start":1981,"end":1990,"place":"Nueva York y Londres","desc":"Tras la caída de P-Funk, muchos ritmos nuevos vinieron de fuera: Kraftwerk, Art of Noise («Beat Box», «Close (to the Edit)»), Talking Heads (Stop Making Sense, con Bernie Worrell) y el «Genius of Love» de Tom Tom Club. Herbie Hancock pidió a su mánager Tony Meilandt unos temas de hip hop y con Bill Laswell y Grandmixer D.S.T. hizo «Rockit» (Future Shock, 1983): la cima del funk industrial nada más empezar. Laswell (Material, Time Zone con John Lydon, Manu Dibango, los Last Poets) dejó su sello de bajo grueso; sellos como Celluloid, On-U Sound, Tommy Boy o Def Jam sacaban ritmos ásperos de raspados metálicos y tensión de Guerra Fría. On-U Sound de Adrian Sherwood fichó a la sección rítmica de Sugarhill (Doug Wimbish, Skip McDonald, Keith LeBlanc): Tackhead, el «No Sell Out» con la voz de Malcolm X, «el P-Funk de los ochenta» según Pedro Bell.","aka":["industrial funk","Tackhead","On-U Sound","Bill Laswell","Rockit"],"notes":[],"influences":[{"name":"Afrika Bambaataa y el electro-funk","id":121508812522429,"type":"genre"},{"name":"Herbie Hancock y los Headhunters","id":234102956257712,"type":"genre"},{"name":"Parliament y la Nave Nodriza","id":158642492229379,"type":"genre"}],"refs":[{"ch":21,"q":"With Bambaataa’s influence, instrumental funk tracks were just as popular as New York rappers by 1984.","para":"With Bambaataa’s influence, instrumental funk tracks were just as popular as New York rappers by 1984. With the demise of the P-Funk Empire in 1980, many of the innovations in funk beats were produced overseas. The German duo known as Kraftwerk was well known to club deejays with their hits “Trans Europe Express,” and the 1981 radio hit “Numbers,” neither of which had any “human” instruments associated with them. The British techno-trio known as Art of Noise hit the country in February 1984 with a loud, stumbling brand of danceable beat tracks, debuting with “Beat Box” and following with one of the all-time industrial funk tracks, “Close (to the Edit),” in the summer.","page":359,"pdf":289,"chapter":"Cap. 20: Hip Hop and Black Noise: Raising Hell"},{"ch":21,"q":"they are the P-Funk of the eighties, they are what P-Funk should have been doing now.","para":"The most prolific purveyors of this new fonk, the On-U-Sound collective, based in London and led by arranger Adrian Sherwood, captured the bleak, tense feel of life in the eighties by hiring the rhythm section of legendary rap label Sugarhill Records: Doug Wimbish on bass, Skip Macdonald on guitar and synthesizers, and Keith LeBlanc on an assortment of drums. The three players called themselves Tackhead and became the rhythm section of the underground—as central to industrial fonk as Sly Dunbar and Robbie Shakespeare were to the eighties reggae sound. With uncredited yet legendary tracks such as “Rapper’s Delight,” “White Lines,” and “The Message” for Sugarhill; the 1984 dub track of Malcolm X, “No Sell Out,” which featured the first samplings of Brother Malcolm’s voice over a (killer) funky beat track; and the phenomenal Artists United Against Apartheid compilation in 1985 all featured the signature grooves of the Tackhead trio. While hits were rare from this unit, their sound was unmistakable, as bassist Wimbish had the voluminous tones to drive rooster-poot fonkiness into the most deliberately technified tracks his buddies could come up with. P-Funk album artist and music critic Pedro Bell summed up the Tackhead sound by saying that “they are the P-Funk of the eighties, they are what P-Funk should have been doing now.”","page":361,"pdf":292,"chapter":"Cap. 20: Hip Hop and Black Noise: Raising Hell"}],"artists":[{"id":30375256435126,"type":"artist","name":"The Talking Heads","rating":null},{"id":156479957595892,"type":"artist","name":"Kraftwerk","rating":null},{"id":162916649169545,"type":"artist","name":"Queen","rating":null},{"id":122533323157799,"type":"artist","name":"Peter Gabriel","rating":null},{"id":265671223542249,"type":"artist","name":"Gary Numan","rating":null},{"id":192413966667751,"type":"artist","name":"John Lydon","rating":null},{"id":44359899745745,"type":"artist","name":"George Clinton","rating":null},{"id":118152760909754,"type":"artist","name":"David Byrne","rating":null},{"id":57041058518848,"type":"artist","name":"Herbie Hancock","rating":null},{"id":180207127356870,"type":"artist","name":"Tom Tom Club","rating":null},{"id":69015807936490,"type":"artist","name":"Manu Dibango","rating":null},{"id":85884620752487,"type":"artist","name":"The B-52's","rating":null},{"id":265519359285159,"type":"artist","name":"The Art of Noise","rating":null},{"id":248355607363799,"type":"artist","name":"Sly & Robbie","rating":null},{"id":140521393044843,"type":"artist","name":"Bill Laswell","rating":null},{